"[quant-ph/0309131] Perfect state transfer in quantum spin networks (https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0309131)\nciteturn83view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0309131\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 159\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Quantum Physics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 17 Sep 2003 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 5 May 2004 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Perfect state transfer in quantum spin networks\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Matthias Christandl , cite7†Nilanjana Datta , cite8†Artur Ekert , cite9†Andrew J. Landahl L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Perfect state transfer in quantum spin networks, by Matthias Christandl and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract: We propose a class of qubit networks that admit perfect transfer of any quantum state in a fixed period of time. Unlike many other schemes for quantum computation and communication, these networks do not require qubit couplings to be switched on and off. When restricted to N-qubit spin networks of identical qubit couplings, we show that 2 log_3 N is the maximal perfect communication distance for hypercube geometries.\nL17: Moreover, if one allows fixed but different couplings between the qubits then perfect state transfer can be achieved over arbitrarily long distances in a linear chain.\nL18: Comments:  | 4 pages, 1 figure\nL19: Subjects:  | Quantum Physics (quant-ph); Condensed Matter (cond-mat)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:quant-ph/0309131 L21:    | (or cite13†arXiv:quant-ph/0309131v2 for this version)\nL22:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.quant-ph/0309131†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: Journal reference:  | Phys. Rev. Lett. 92, 187902 (2004)\nL24: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite15†https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.92.187902†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Matthias Christandl [cite16†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Wed, 17 Sep 2003 15:27:37 UTC (12 KB)\nL29: [v2] Wed, 5 May 2004 00:14:01 UTC (11 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Perfect state transfer in quantum spin networks, by Matthias Christandl and 2 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite10†View PDF L38:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite17†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite18†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: quant-ph\nL45: \nL46: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L47: \nL48: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2003-09 L49: \nL50: ### References & Citations\nL51: \nL52:   * cite24†INSPIRE HEP†inspirehep.net L53:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L54:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L55:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L56: \nL57: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL58: \nL59: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL60: \nL61: [Button: ×]\nL62: \nL63: loading...\nL64: \nL65: Data provided by:\nL66: \nL67: ### Bookmark\nL68: \nL69: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL70: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL73: \nL74: Bibliographic Explorer (cite28†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL75: \nL76: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL77: \nL78: Connected Papers (cite29†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL79: \nL80: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL81: \nL82: Litmaps (cite30†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL83: \nL84: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL85: \nL86: scite Smart Citations (cite31†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL87: \nL88: [Input] Code, Data, Media\nL89: # 